<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8' standalone='yes' ?>
<!--
  Nowhere (NWP-20260827-23): pre-grant the calendar RUNTIME permissions to Etar (ws.xsoh.etar), the
  bundled calendar app.

  WHY THIS IS NEEDED: Nowhere mints a FRESH Android user on every login, and runtime grants are
  per-(package, user). Without a default-permissions exception, Etar starts every single login with
  READ_CALENDAR denied, so the calendar the first-login ingest just wrote into CalendarProvider renders
  EMPTY until the user grants it again, and grants it again on the next login, and the one after that.
  That is the exact failure Etar was bundled to fix (NWP-20260827-22): before Etar there was no calendar
  UI at all, so imported .ics events landed correctly in the provider and were invisible. An ungranted
  Etar reproduces the same symptom with an extra step.

  Same mechanism and same reasoning as the geotz location grant (nowhere-geotz-location.xml): a first-boot
  `pm grant` only ever covers user 0, whereas a default-permissions exception applies at EVERY user's
  creation, which is where a roamed session actually runs. Etar is a SYSTEM package (/product/app/Etar),
  so the exception applies to it.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Etar also requests READ_CONTACTS (attendee autocomplete) and
  GET_ACCOUNTS (account-backed calendars). Both are left on demand: they reach OUTSIDE the calendar into
  other user data, and neither is needed to display or edit a local roamed calendar. Only the permissions
  the app needs to do its own job on its own data are pre-granted.

  fixed="false" leaves every grant user-revocable, matching the other exceptions in this directory.
  -->
<exceptions>
    <exception package="ws.xsoh.etar">
        <permission name="android.permission.READ_CALENDAR" fixed="false"/>
        <permission name="android.permission.WRITE_CALENDAR" fixed="false"/>
        <!-- Event reminders are a core calendar behaviour; without this every login silently drops them. -->
        <permission name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" fixed="false"/>
    </exception>
</exceptions>
